  :adr/body "## Two reports, one screen

2026-09-07. The echo on submit showed the same sentence twice:

    ❯ 今の itonami bots profile に…bot profile は今の itonami bots profile に…bot profile は

and the answer was 「考える回数の上限に達したので、ここで止めます」.

## The doubling was mine, and it was not the echo

Typing did not redraw the block. At all.

The key loop redrew only on the branches that happened to remember, and when
the input loop was separated from the turn (ADR-2609071400) the ordinary branch
-- a character -- stopped being one of them. Measured by driving a pty and
sending one `X`: the last bytes on the wire are the initial frame, and the `X`
never reaches the screen.

So nothing appeared while the operator typed, they typed the line again, and
the buffer really did hold it twice. The echo was correct; it printed what was
there.

## Why nothing caught it

Every pty check written for the input area ran a slash command or a run in the
same breath, and both redraw through other paths (`say!`, the progress tick).
Not one of them typed a character, changed nothing else, and looked.

That is the shape CLAUDE.md's fifth question asks about -- a check that has
only ever been run in a state where something else would have made it pass.
The fix is not only the missing redraw:

`editor/step` now takes a chunk of keys and returns
`{:state :submits :signal :redraw?}`. Whether the screen has to change is a
VALUE a test can read, rather than a branch a loop has to remember. The loop in
`bin/itonami` reads it. Control: forcing `:redraw?` to false fails
`typing-asks-for-a-redraw` by name.

A chunk that changes nothing still redraws. One frame is cheap, and deciding
which keys are invisible is the decision that was got wrong.

Two more things `step` pins, both of which the old loop got right by accident
and nothing asserted: every submit in one chunk is carried out (a fast typist
and a paste both arrive as one read), and a signal stops the chunk there (keys
behind a Ctrl+D must not be applied to a session that is going away).

## The ceiling had no knob

`考える回数の上限` is `bots/max-turns`, a constant 8. `bot_bounds/cap` only ever
NARROWS -- that is what a ceiling is for -- so the per-Bot `:bot/max-turns`
added on 2026-09-06 could lower it and nothing could raise it. Goal mode already
read its tool-call ceiling from configuration; the interactive ceilings read
from nowhere.

They now read `[:bots :max-turns]` and `[:bots :max-tool-calls]`, with the
constants as the default, and both are declared in the shipped defaults so they
are discoverable rather than folklore. Per-Bot narrowing is unchanged: a Bot
still cannot raise its own ceiling.

The operator config is raised to 24 turns and 40 tool calls (owner instruction
「もっと試行回数を増やして」): the turn ceiling matches goal mode's, and the tool
ceiling has headroom because the workspace tools are cheap and one search costs
several calls. The previous file is kept beside it as a dated backup.

## What this does not claim

A ceiling that is reached is still evidence about the work, not only about the
ceiling. The turn that prompted this ran eight `workspace_list` calls looking
for something the Bot has no tool to read -- its six tools are filesystem and
git. Twenty-four turns will let it search longer; it will not let it answer
that question. The Bot's tool set is the thing to change for that, and it is
not changed here.
"}]
